  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Bio-methanol in Zimbabwe</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Solid Hydrogen B.V. (SH) develops innovative services and technologies for the energy transition, especially in shipping. According to SH, methanol is seen as one of the most promising options as a green fuel suitable for shipping. Cheap biomass as a raw material is required to make bio-methanol. Zimbabwe is a country with an enormous maize production and therefore a country with sufficient biomass. The edible part of the corn (the primary biomass) is harvested, but the vast majority is burned by farmers. The secondary part is currently still a low-quality residual product with which a lot of value can be created by using this biomass through a gasification process. This technology and process is already available and has been used in the chemical industry for many years.SH wants to invest in a small-scale factory in Zimbabwe that will produce bio-methanol that can be used by the shipping industry to sail CO2 neutrally. By first investing in a small-scale production facility and demonstrating that a reliable supply chain can be set up, and a high-quality (green) fuel can be produced, bio-methanol may be produced in Zimbabwe on a much larger scale in the longer term.With this project, SH wants to demonstrate that it can set up a robust supply chain on a small scale and prove that there is a strong business case for bio-methanol from secondary biomass from Zimbabwe.</narrative>
  </description>
